WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:08.350
As of 2015 Planet Earth is home to 7 billion
people but the population is estimated to

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:10.670
reach up to 11 billion by 2050.

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:15.099
That’s an increase of 4 billion people in
just 35 years.

00:00:15.099 --> 00:00:19.610
But researchers warn that planet Earth cannot
sustain 11 billion people.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:20.610
Is this true?

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:25.990
Is there a limit to how many people can reasonably
live on planet Earth and if so what is that

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:26.990
limit?

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:32.010
Let’s find out.

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:36.800
In the past 50 years the human population
has increased exponentially.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:43.820
It took humans 150,000 years for the population
to reach one billion, in 1850.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:48.719
Then only 100 years later in 1950 the population
hit 2.5 billion.

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:53.440
It then took just 60 more years to reach 7
billion.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.089
Astonishingly, at the current rate of growth,
the planet’s population increases by one

00:00:58.089 --> 00:01:00.449
billion every 12 years.

00:01:00.449 --> 00:01:05.500
This phenomenal growth is mostly due to one
thing, advances in medicine.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:07.900
The invention of Penicillin for example.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:12.480
But each and every one of those seven billion
people requires a minimum amount of food,

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:18.110
water, light, shelter, clothing, education,
medicine and other things to live an adequate

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:19.390
standard of life.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:24.430
But food doesn’t come from nowhere, it has
to be farmed and farming takes space, lots

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:25.990
and lots of space.

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:30.990
A staggering 40% of the Earth’s surface
is now taken over by farmland.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:36.620
That’s a lot; when you consider that in
the 1700s that figure was just 7%.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:43.230
But how much of that arable land is needed
to farm enough food to sustain just one human

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:44.230
being.

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:47.640
That number is 2.1 hectares.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.930
It takes 2.1 hectares of land and water to
provide for the average human being.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:57.160
But that’s the average human and the world
is far from average.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:03.560
The average European uses 4.5 hectares but
America leads the world in food and water

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:04.560
consumption.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.380
The average American uses 10 hectares of land.

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:14.680
However the world only has 4.1 billion hectares
of arable land and the vast majority of it

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:19.189
that can be realistically farmed, is already
being farmed.

00:02:19.189 --> 00:02:21.799
There is very little left to go around.

00:02:21.799 --> 00:02:28.879
Based on the worldwide average of 2.1 hectares
for each person we currently need 1.5 Earths

00:02:28.879 --> 00:02:31.830
to sustain the current population of 7 billion.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:35.049
And that’s based on the average level of
consumption.

00:02:35.049 --> 00:02:39.989
If the whole world, all seven billion people,
lived at a European standard, we would need

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:43.519
3.4 Earths to sustain everyone.

00:02:43.519 --> 00:02:48.480
If however, the entire world’s population
consumed the average American quantity of

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.930
food and water, we would need 5 Earths.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:57.840
So it’s fair to say, that we’re well beyond
capacity, which partially explains why over

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.969
one billion people worldwide don’t have
access to regular food or clean water.

00:03:02.969 --> 00:03:06.450
It’s not the only reason, but a considerable
factor.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.180
So how many people can the Earth sustain?

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:13.390
Well, about one-third less than it currently
is.

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:18.220
The unfortunate truth is that for developed
countries to live the way they currently do,

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:21.249
the global consumption of food has to be unbalanced.

00:03:21.249 --> 00:03:26.620
Sadly, a vast portion of the population has
to starve for the rest of the world to gorge

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:31.959
themselves on a daily basis, because there
quite simply isn’t enough food and water

00:03:31.959 --> 00:03:32.959
to go around.

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:37.670
If you think that’s bad, it’s nothing
compared to the problems we would face if

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:40.999
the population doubled from what it is now.

00:03:40.999 --> 00:03:46.209
Currently one-third of all the food produced
in the world on a daily basis is wasted.

00:03:46.209 --> 00:03:48.079
It gets lost or thrown away.

00:03:48.079 --> 00:03:52.859
It is estimated that if we could be totally
efficient and don’t waste any food, operating

00:03:52.859 --> 00:03:57.099
at maximum capacity, the Earth could support
up to 10 billion people.

00:03:57.099 --> 00:04:02.370
But there’s a catch, to feed 10 billion
people, not only would we have to stop wasting

00:04:02.370 --> 00:04:07.689
food but the entire world would need to go
vegetarian.

00:04:07.689 --> 00:04:11.750
Farming livestock is a horrendously inefficient
way to produce food.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.480
You have to grow crops to feed to the livestock.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:19.019
A significant portion of the crops produced
today aren’t for feeding humans, they’re

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:23.000
for feeding animals that will end up on our
dinner plates.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:28.430
So to feed 10 billion people this inefficient
method of food production would have to completely

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:30.150
cease.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:35.610
But that’s no fun, everyone loves chicken,
well not everyone, some people really, really

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:37.090
don’t like chicken.

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:43.780
And then there’s health concerns, an entirely
vegetarian diet doesn’t provide enough protein

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:48.480
and humans are designed to eat protein, it
serves innumerable essential functions in

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:54.169
our bodies such as repairing muscles, making
enzymes and hormones, and other things.

00:04:54.169 --> 00:04:58.990
Generally speaking, protein is a crucial building
block of bones, muscles, skin, cartilage and

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:00.020
blood.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:04.729
Normally vegetarians replace the lack of protein
in their diet with foods such as eggs and

00:05:04.729 --> 00:05:05.729
cheese.

00:05:05.729 --> 00:05:11.390
But all those require farmed animals to produce
on a large scale, so in a world of 10 billion

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:14.770
people, those foods are no longer an option.

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:19.039
That leaves very few sources of protein, mainly
tofu, nuts and seeds.

00:05:19.039 --> 00:05:25.100
Can you just imagine trying to convince the
whole world to eat only tofu, nuts, seeds,

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:28.190
fruit, vegetables, grains and potatoes?

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:32.060
Nope I can’t either, it just wouldn’t
work.

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:33.060
So what do we do?

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:35.129
What’s the ideal solution?

00:05:35.129 --> 00:05:40.670
Based on current estimates the population
in 2050 could be as high as 11 billion, way

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:43.580
beyond what the Earth can reasonably support.

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:46.169
There are three ways to solve this problem.

00:05:46.169 --> 00:05:48.240
One; reduce the population.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:51.449
Two; vastly reduce our consumption of resources.

00:05:51.449 --> 00:05:57.389
Or three; improve our current technology to
make farming significantly more productive,

00:05:57.389 --> 00:06:02.690
so each plot of farmland produces double the
amount of food it currently does.

00:06:02.690 --> 00:06:07.050
Doubling the productivity sounds like a lot
but research has shown that is what would

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.870
be required to feed 10 billion.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:12.240
So there is hope.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:17.849
Doubling our productivity sounds unachievable,
but based on history it can be achieved.

00:06:17.849 --> 00:06:23.060
In the 17th century industrialised nations
managed to triple their farming productivity

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:27.340
by inventing new types of grain and mechanising
farming techniques.

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:30.530
So who knows what the future of farming technology
holds?

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:35.660
It may not sound appetising but in an over-populated
world we may even have to resort to eating

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:38.080
synthesised food grown in a lab.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:41.639
But don’t start stocking up on tins of beans
just yet.

00:06:41.639 --> 00:06:47.030
Sit down and chill, because there’s new
research that suggests we may never get to

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:48.449
10 billion people.

00:06:48.449 --> 00:06:52.610
Statistics show that in the developed world
the average number of children families are

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:54.330
having is declining.

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:57.530
But in Africa it’s increasing.

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:02.280
This map shows the average number of children
families in every country has.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:09.879
The average for most of Europe is 1-2 children,
America 2-3 children and thanks to their controversial

00:07:09.879 --> 00:07:15.860
one child per family policy, China also has
an average of 1-2 per family.

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:21.800
Some countries in Africa on the other hand
have an average of 6-7 children per family.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:22.800
Why is that?

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:26.090
Well, it has little to do with a desire for
more children.

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:29.030
The main problem is a lack of contraception.

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:34.759
Over half of all Africans would like to use
birth control but only 30% of the country

00:07:34.759 --> 00:07:36.449
has access to it.

00:07:36.449 --> 00:07:42.389
As you can see on this map of worldwide contraception
usage, in many African countries only 6% of

00:07:42.389 --> 00:07:46.139
the population has access to or uses birth
control.

00:07:46.139 --> 00:07:51.289
I’m by no means blaming Africans themselves
for this; the problem lies with politics,

00:07:51.289 --> 00:07:56.949
economics and to a smaller extent education
and commonly held religious beliefs surrounding

00:07:56.949 --> 00:07:58.330
contraception.

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:02.629
So what’s the ideal number of children to
stop the worldwide population spiralling out

00:08:02.629 --> 00:08:05.060
of control in 30 years time?

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:07.439
2.1 children per family.

00:08:07.439 --> 00:08:11.580
That is what is known as the fertility “replacement
level”.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:17.370
If the average number of children every family
had in the world dropped to 2.1 then the population

00:08:17.370 --> 00:08:19.250
would never increase.

00:08:19.250 --> 00:08:22.990
Because two children is just enough to replace
their parents when they pass on.

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:28.169
If the whole world averaged 2.1 children per
family the global population would stabilise

00:08:28.169 --> 00:08:30.129
at its current level.

00:08:30.129 --> 00:08:34.039
There is some proof that this is actually
happening right now.

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:38.229
The average number of children per family,
globally, is decreasing.

00:08:38.229 --> 00:08:42.880
Studies by the United Nations show that the
fertility rate in the majority of countries

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:44.850
is slowly decreasing.

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:47.089
So we may never reach 10 billion.

00:08:47.089 --> 00:08:51.980
Some optimistic estimates predict the population
will stabilise at around 9 billion.

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:57.730
If we be sensible about our consumption of
resources and prevent waste wherever possible;

00:08:57.730 --> 00:09:01.660
then the Earth can absolutely support 9 billion
people.

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:02.660
Just.

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:05.500
Who knows what will happen in 30 years time.

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:10.850
But there are things we can do now to prevent
a global famine in the future.

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:15.149
We can help and education less developed nations
and provide better health care and access

00:09:15.149 --> 00:09:17.120
to birth control.

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:22.000
Developed countries can vastly reduce their
resource consumption and prevent waste and

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:27.730
most importantly, we can care for and preserve
the Earth’s natural resources instead of

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:31.000
carelessly destroying them for profits.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:38.980
Because nature, more than anything else, is
supporting human life, as we know it.

